--- body ---
## Context

R32's build bound is a rate on the whole pipeline — extract, chunk and embed —
pinned in `SPEC.md` §5.2.8 at 150 ms per passage as a MUST and 75 ms as a
SHOULD on the reference machine. Only one of the three terms is measured. Embed
has `bench/results/0025-x1-recall/embed-feasibility.json` and the CPU cells
ticket 0481 recovered from `bench/results/0264-gpu-arm/`; extract and chunk have
nothing in this repository at all, so their share of the budget is an allocation
written to be re-cut rather than a finding.

The allocation stands at 30 ms for the two of them at the MUST and 10 ms at the
SHOULD. It was chosen so the total holds with the measured embed rates, not
because anyone knows what extraction costs. That is exactly the shape the
pin-from-measurement rule exists to retire.

Two things are known without measuring. Extraction is usually a read of the
platform's own full-text cache rather than a parse — the census counted 13 630
caches — so the common path is I/O. The expensive path is the attachment the
platform has not indexed, where a 15k-page PDF yields tens of MiB of text
(ticket 0480), and that path is also where the cap in ticket 0483 bites.

## Actions

1. Time the extract stage on the reference machine over the two paths
   separately: cache hit, and on-the-fly parse. Report ms per passage for each
   and the mix observed on a real library, since the mix is what decides the
   effective rate.
2. Time the chunk stage over the same corpus, including the tokenizer pass the
   passage census already runs — that pass is the cost proxy and its wall clock
   was not recorded when the census ran.
3. Report both against the passage-length distribution they ran on, because a
   rate does not transfer across distributions (§2.8) and ticket 0029 pins the
   fixture's.
4. Re-cut §2.8's allocation from the measurements, or report that the total
   cannot hold with the measured embed rates, which would be a finding about the
   bound rather than about the stages.

## Measurement, 2026-09-02, on the reference machine

Ran by `bench/extract_chunk_throughput.mjs`; artifact
`bench/results/0500-extract-chunk/extract-chunk-throughput.json`. Host doudou,
Intel i5-8250U at 1,6 GHz, node v22.23.1 — the machine SPEC.md §5.2.8 names.
The chunker under measurement is imported from the built `dist` the server runs,
not reimplemented, so the number cannot drift from the code it claims to measure.

**Five repetitions over disjoint slices**, 80 items each, so no repetition reads
an item an earlier one warmed. One pass is not a rate: four single-pass runs put
the extract term between 0,14 and 0,26 ms per passage, and the median over
disjoint slices is what the artifact quotes. Load average was 3,72 throughout —
the machine was not idle, and the tight spread below is itself the evidence that
this term is bound by a loopback read of the desktop application rather than by
CPU.

**What the build's extract stage actually is.** `build.ts` gets body text from
`createFulltextSource`, whose `textFor` is a GET of the platform's own
`/items/<key>/fulltext`. The population it can serve is exactly what
`/fulltext?since=0` names, so an attachment the platform has not extracted is not
in the list, the source cannot serve it, and the build never asks. The mix is
therefore a property of the architecture, not of the library: **every extract a
build performs is a cache hit, 8 037 of 8 037**. Measured beside it, on this
library the parse path has no instance either — of 9 302 attachments, 59 PDFs
carry no platform text and **all 59 are `linked_url`**, with no local file to
parse. That count is a census of content types and link modes, not an empty
filter: a filter that finds nothing and one that cannot look return the same
list.

| stage | ms per passage, median of 5 | min–max |
|---|---|---|
| extract, one request in flight | **0,142** | 0,137-0,145 |
| chunk (`chunkText`, 1200/150) | **0,022** | 0,020-0,029 |
| extract + chunk, serial | **0,164** | 0,157-0,174 |
| extract + chunk, at the build's own local-API concurrency of 2 | **0,122** | one pass |

377 items read over the five slices, **22 562 passages**. Passage length: median
**1 197** characters, p95 **1 200**, mean **1 187,5** — the distribution the rate
ran on, since a rate does not transfer across distributions. The concurrent arm
ran on a sixth, disjoint slice, so the pooled figure is not a warm cache read as
a concurrency gain.

**The parse path, priced out of the critical path.** Forcing re-extraction
through the control plugin (`Zotero.FullText.indexItems` with `complete: true`,
which re-parses whether or not a cache exists) over four PDFs spread across the
library: **10,08 ms per passage** median, from 3,45 on a 198-page document to
27,76 on a 5-page one — the per-passage cost falls with document size, which is
what a fixed per-file overhead over a variable yield looks like. This is the
platform's cost, paid once when a user first opens a PDF, and it is never inside
the build's own clock.

**The fixed term, which the per-passage rate hides.** Before any passage is read,
the source lists the full-text sequence (105,5 ms) and walks the attachment pages
to map each extracted attachment to its parent: **80,6 s** on this library, 94
pages of 100. That is one-time per build, and it is exactly the fixed work
§5.2.8 warns a rate conceals. Amortised, it is **21,8 ms per passage on the
60-item derisking build** (3 699 passages) and **0,17 ms per passage on a
full-library build** (~465k passages). A sample can therefore fail a rate the
real build meets, which is the opposite of the usual worry about samples.

**Extract work that yields no passage.** Of 80 sampled items in the first slice,
6 failed the read (404 on an attachment the sequence names) and 1 answered with
an empty body — the version-0 entries. Across the five slices, 23 of 400.
Counted, not dropped: they are extract-stage work that produces nothing, so they
raise the effective rate rather than lower it.

**The tokenizer pass, and why it is not in this term.** The census tokenizes; the
build's chunker does not — `chunkText` splits on characters and word boundaries,
with no tokenizer anywhere in the module. Measured anyway, since the census's
wall clock was never recorded when it ran: **0,641 ms per passage**, **288,2**
tokens per passage over 1 242 272 tokens. That cost sits inside embed, not
inside this allocation.

**What was not measured.** The record write, which §5.2.8's 30 ms covers together
with extract and chunk, is not isolated here: it lives inside the index manager's
`putPassage` and its persistence, and pricing it needs a built index rather than
a source read. Reported as not-run rather than folded into the two stages that
were measured. The full-library build launched the same night is the end-to-end
confirmation this pass cannot give.

**Provenance of the committed artifact, stated rather than glossed.** After the
run, the driver was corrected to satisfy ticket 0260's guard: it now issues a
discarded warm-up pass before the clock, and times each stage in one window per
repetition instead of summing a clock read per item. The committed artifact was
produced by the pass *before* that correction, because the full-library build
took ownership of Zotero's local API minutes later and re-running would have
loaded it — the exact condition that degrades a build to one-at-a-time fetches
and never recovers. What the correction can be worth is bounded by the run
itself: the five disjoint repetitions read 0,145 / 0,139 / 0,143 / 0,142 / 0,137
ms per passage, the coldest first, so the first-connection cost the warm-up
removes is at most about 0,008 ms per passage — three orders of magnitude below
the allocation being re-cut. A confirming re-run belongs after the build.

## The re-cut

§5.2.8 says the allocation across stages is provisional and the total is not, and
that the split may be re-cut in any proportion so long as the total holds. The
two stages that were unpinned are now pinned at **0,164 ms per passage together**
— about half a percent of the 30 ms they were allocated. The allocation is
re-cut to **5 ms at the MUST and 2 ms at the SHOULD** for extract, chunk and the
record write together, which leaves the unmeasured record write more than an
order of magnitude of room above the two measured stages, and gives embed
**145 ms** and **73 ms**.

R32's own 150 ms and 75 ms are untouched. Nothing measured here asks them to
move, and moving them would be the author's ruling rather than this ticket's.

One derived observation, labelled derived and not acted on. The derisking build
of 2026-09-02 ran 3 699 passages in 506 s, i.e. 137 ms per passage end to end. If
extract and chunk cost 0,16 ms of that and the attachment map 21,8 ms, the
remainder sits near 115 ms — inside the old 120 ms embed allocation. But that is
a subtraction from somebody else's total, not a measurement of embed, and the
full-library build is what will say.

## Exit criteria
- [x] extract measured on both paths, with the observed mix, on the reference machine
- [x] chunk measured on the same corpus and distribution
- [x] both figures committed under `bench/results/` and declared in `bench/check_figures.py`
- [x] §2.8's per-stage allocation re-cut from measurement, or the bound revised
